the last two days it's been almost 3 000  cases per day and what you have to do is  put that against the backdrop  of the previous seven days  where on each of those days the kind of  case  counts we've seen through testing have  been in the range of  shall we say 1400 to 1700.  so this is a big change it's now  consistent over two days  and it is of great concern at this point  i think you know we've been able to  relax a bit over the summer the disease  levels have been  really quite low in the uk through the  summer  but these latest figures  really show us that um much as people  might like to say oh well it's gone away  this hasn't gone away this is a virus  we're going to have to live with  and if we're not careful if we don't  take this  incredibly seriously from this point in  um we're going to have a bumpy ride over  the next few months  what is the reason do you think for such  a considerable increase  well people have relaxed too much people  have relaxed too much  and um now is the time um  for us to re-engage and realize that  this  is a continuing threat to us um  it's all very well saying that hospital  admissions and deaths  are at a very low level in the uk which  is true  but if you look further into the  european union  you can see that where case numbers rise  initially in the younger parts of the  population  they do in turn filter through and start  to give  elevated rates of disease and hospital  admissions  in the older age groups and we know that  that then becomes  a serious public health problem so  that's my concern that if we don't get  on top of this  if people don't start to take this  seriously again  then um there is a risk that that's  where we will end up  and let's be clear that the epidemiology  is very clear that there is a rise in  all age groups  but it is really really much more marked  in the 17 to 21 years age group  than any other and for the first time  i think i see a trend that  this is no longer about disease in those  specific  hot spots such as leicester a few months  ago  the north west more recently but there  is a more general  and creeping geographic trend across the  uk  across england that disease levels  are now beginning to turn up on a wider  basis  wider geographical basis and that again  is really a signal that we've got to  change this now  got to start taking it seriously very  seriously again  do you think there may need to be a  tightening  of some of the restrictions that were  eased  this is very new news i think public  health policy officials  and politicians are going to need a  little time  to absorb this but they are going to  have to think about  how we manage this um and how we do so  not in a short-term way not about the  next six days  not about the next six weeks but about  the next six  months and how we get through this until  the spring  so would you say at least in your view  there shouldn't be any further easing  in the next six months i would say that  we've really got to take this very  seriously now  um it is clear that the level of  compliance  with the advice and the rules that have  been set  is very variable indeed and indeed i  heard another of your  interviewees comment on um  people organizing parties and gatherings  and so forth  um you know of course we all want to do  this kind of thing  but the downstream consequences are that  we get  increased transmission and i am you know  quite concerned that we must take this  seriously so that we don't end up  with a spreading problem over the next  few weeks and months  and just just finally the message to  younger people who might feel they're  being stigmatized or blamed here  is the risk of spreading it  inadvertently to older members of the  population is that right  if the data and it's all about the data  said  that the problem was in  people in in england aged 50 to 59  not heeding the advice and the disease  rates were climbing  extraordinarily in that particular age  group  i'd be very quick to point the finger at  the 50 to 59 year olds  i'm not pointing the finger at anybody  i'm telling you  telling you all and all your listeners  what the data show  and the data show that the particular  problem  is currently in the 17 to 21 year age  group  and is that because of gatherings  basically  in people's houses because of close  social contact um this disease thrives  unfortunately on the things we love  doing which is getting together  and being social beings which is what we  are  it is just very unfortunate that that is  so  but when we break those  social distancing rules particularly in  enclosed spaces  and particularly with conditions of  overcrowding  then we get rapidly into situations of  rapid  disease transmission and just finally  explain  again to the or general audience why if  it's 17 to 20 or one-year-olds who  probably won't get seriously ill  there is a particular problem for why  does society and why it leads to  hospitalizations  17 to 21 year olds  in company with really all other  adult age groups shed the virus  quite efficiently and so the fact that  17 to 21 year olds  are not becoming ill means they're lucky  because of their age but it also and  they forget that because the disease  is not severe for them that they are not  very potent spreaders  and after these parties they go home to  their more elderly parents  a couple of days later they  when they're infectious but not yet  having symptoms  they go and visit grandparents and  that's how the problem begins  we get this spread into the older  segments of families and once it gets  there as you know  as we all know from the previous  experiences of march april and may  that it then becomes a very serious  problem  that takes a long while to sort out  and i'll ask one final one if i may some  are saying well hang on  hospitals are much better at dealing  with sicker patients  in intensive care  it's being managed better there's a lot  more testing  it shouldn't be as